<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<bpmn:definitions xmlns:bpmn="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL"
                  xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI"
                  xmlns:dc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC"
                  xmlns:di="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI"
                  xmlns:folio="http://folio-assistant.dev/bpmn"
                  id="Definitions_SessionStateMachine"
                  targetNamespace="http://folio-assistant.dev/bpmn/session-state-machine">

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       An agent PLAYING a state machine — folio-assistant
       Bean: folio-assistant-3nfv

       The owner, 2026-09-20: "an agent could also play a
       (non-deterministic) state machine as a tool. managing state
       (kinda) during human/agentic discussion. this should be a
       workflow."

       WHERE THE NON-DETERMINISM LIVES, and it is not where it looks.
       A machine whose SHAPE were non-deterministic could not be drawn:
       the interpreter supports nine element types and throws on the
       rest, deliberately. So the shape here is strict and enumerable,
       and what varies is WHICH ENABLED BRANCH THE AGENT TAKES and what
       it writes. That distinction is the whole design, and it needed no
       change to the engine — an exclusive gateway with no
       `folio:decision` was ALREADY the case where the caller supplies
       the outcome.

       WHAT DID NEED ADDING is `<folio:judgement reason="…"/>`. Without
       it, "no table because this is somebody's call" and "no table
       because nobody wrote one yet" are indistinguishable — the gap
       `folio:no-skill` closed for activities, one element type along.
       Every gateway below carries it, because every one of them is a
       reading of intent that no table can make.

       THE MACHINE CLAIMS NOTHING. No `folio:bean` anywhere: this
       process RECORDS that an actor claimed a bean, it does not claim
       one. A machine that quietly claimed on the actor's behalf would
       make the claim's owner unrecoverable, and a claim ANNOUNCES
       rather than reserves precisely so a reader can tell who
       announced it.

       ADVISORY, not strict. An agent keeping its own session record
       must never be refused — a missing update is a gap in a record,
       not an unauthorised write. The base content processes gate; this
       one describes.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <bpmn:collaboration id="Collab_SessionStateMachine">
    <bpmn:participant id="Participant_SessionStateMachine" name="Session state machine" processRef="Process_SessionStateMachine"/>
  </bpmn:collaboration>

  <bpmn:process id="Process_SessionStateMachine" name="Session state machine" isExecutable="true">
    <bpmn:extensionElements>
      <folio:policy enforcement="advisory"/>
    </bpmn:extensionElements>

    <bpmn:laneSet id="LaneSet_SessionStateMachine">
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_Machine" name="Agent (playing the machine)">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_Session</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_IdentifyActor</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Gateway_ActorKnown</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_OpenSession</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_ReadTurn</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Gateway_TurnEffect</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_Touch</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_UpdateSession</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_CloseSession</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_Session</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_Human" name="Human actor">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>A_AskWhoIsActing</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <!-- Written to, decides nothing. No flowNodeRef, which is what
           `actedUpon` means. -->
      <bpmn:lane id="Lane_Record" name="Session record"/>
    </bpmn:laneSet>

    <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_Session" name="An actor picks up">
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_1</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:startEvent>

    <bpmn:task id="A_IdentifyActor" name="Establish who is acting">
      <bpmn:documentation>The first step and not a formality: `actor` is the one field of the record that nothing else can supply. Every other field is recoverable by reading the repository; who is acting is not.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="session-context"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_1</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_4</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_2</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Gateway_ActorKnown" name="Actor established?">
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <folio:judgement reason="Only the discussion says who is acting, and it rarely says so in a field. A table would need a name to read; this branch is the reading."/>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_2</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_3</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_5</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>

    <bpmn:userTask id="A_AskWhoIsActing" name="Ask who is acting">
      <bpmn:documentation>The one step that needs a person. Asked ONCE and recorded, never re-asked: `interaction/interaction.json` and the record exist so that a preference stated once is not requested again, which is WCAG 2.2 SC 3.3.7.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="interaction-modality"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_3</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_4</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:userTask>

    <bpmn:task id="A_OpenSession" name="Open the session record">
      <bpmn:documentation>Writes `id`, `actor` and `startedAt`, with `open` and `claimed` empty. An empty `open` is the NORMAL state, not an unfinished one: an agent reading, answering or deciding is in no process at all.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="session-context"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_5</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_6</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:task id="A_ReadTurn" name="Take in the turn">
      <bpmn:documentation>A turn is anything that arrives — a person's message, a tool result, a notification. The machine advances on turns rather than on a clock, because a session that has been told nothing has not changed.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <folio:no-skill reason="reading what a turn said is the agent's ordinary comprehension, not a procedure an instruction body could add anything to"/>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_6</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_9</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_11</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_7</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Gateway_TurnEffect" name="What did the turn change?">
      <bpmn:documentation>THE non-deterministic point, and the reason this process exists. Three branches, and the agent chooses.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <folio:judgement reason="Whether a turn changed the session's state is a reading of intent — 'I might look at X later' and 'start X' differ by nothing a table can key on. Getting it wrong is recoverable: the next turn corrects it, which is why this is judgement rather than a gate."/>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_7</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_8</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_10</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_12</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>

    <bpmn:task id="A_Touch" name="Refresh `updatedAt` only">
      <bpmn:documentation>Nothing changed, and saying so is the point. A record that stops being written looks exactly like an abandoned session; a refreshed timestamp with no other change says the session is alive and idle, which is a different fact and the commonest one.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="session-context"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_8</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_9</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:task id="A_UpdateSession" name="Write what changed">
      <bpmn:documentation>An instance opened or closed, a bean claimed, a wait started or ended. All by REFERENCE: the bean and the instance are each the authority on themselves, and a status copied here would be free to contradict them.

NO `folio:bean` on this step. It records that a claim happened; it does not claim.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="session-context"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_10</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_11</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:task id="A_CloseSession" name="Close the session">
      <bpmn:documentation>Records that the actor stopped. It does NOT resolve beans or complete instances: those outlive the session by design, and a machine that closed them on the way out would be asserting work finished because somebody went away.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:extensionElements><folio:skill ref="session-context"/></bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>F_12</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>F_13</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>

    <bpmn:endEvent id="End_Session" name="Session closed">
      <bpmn:incoming>F_13</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>

    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_1" sourceRef="Start_Session" targetRef="A_IdentifyActor"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_2" sourceRef="A_IdentifyActor" targetRef="Gateway_ActorKnown"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_3" name="no" sourceRef="Gateway_ActorKnown" targetRef="A_AskWhoIsActing"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_4" sourceRef="A_AskWhoIsActing" targetRef="A_IdentifyActor"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_5" name="yes" sourceRef="Gateway_ActorKnown" targetRef="A_OpenSession"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_6" sourceRef="A_OpenSession" targetRef="A_ReadTurn"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_7" sourceRef="A_ReadTurn" targetRef="Gateway_TurnEffect"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_8" name="nothing changed" sourceRef="Gateway_TurnEffect" targetRef="A_Touch"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_9" sourceRef="A_Touch" targetRef="A_ReadTurn"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_10" name="state changed" sourceRef="Gateway_TurnEffect" targetRef="A_UpdateSession"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_11" sourceRef="A_UpdateSession" targetRef="A_ReadTurn"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_12" name="the session is ending" sourceRef="Gateway_TurnEffect" targetRef="A_CloseSession"/>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="F_13" sourceRef="A_CloseSession" targetRef="End_Session"/>
  </bpmn:process>
</bpmn:definitions>
